/* ============================================================
   SECTION 2: pd.merge
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">2. pd.merge -- SQL-Style Joins</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>pd.merge() matches rows by key columns</strong>, exactly like SQL JOIN. This is the most important combining operation -- it lets you enrich one dataset with information from another based on shared identifiers (user IDs, product codes, timestamps).</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">The four join types determine what happens when keys exist in one table but not the other:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare"><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">Inner Join (default)</div><div class="compare-item">Only rows with matching keys in BOTH tables</div><div class="compare-item">Result is smaller or equal to each input</div><div class="compare-item">No NaN values from the join itself</div><div class="compare-item">Use when: you only want complete records</div></div><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">Left Join</div><div class="compare-item">ALL rows from left table, matching from right</div><div class="compare-item">Unmatched right rows \u2192 NaN</div><div class="compare-item">Result has same row count as left table</div><div class="compare-item">Use when: left table is your primary dataset</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare"><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">Right Join</div><div class="compare-item">ALL rows from right table, matching from left</div><div class="compare-item">Unmatched left rows \u2192 NaN</div><div class="compare-item">Symmetric to left join with swapped inputs</div><div class="compare-item">Use when: right table is primary (rare)</div></div><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">Outer Join</div><div class="compare-item">ALL rows from BOTH tables</div><div class="compare-item">Unmatched rows on either side \u2192 NaN</div><div class="compare-item">Result is largest possible</div><div class="compare-item">Use when: you need the complete picture</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># --- Setup: Two related DataFrames ---</span>\ntexts = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"doc_id"</span>: [<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">5</span>],\n    <span class="str">"text"</span>: [<span class="str">"Great film"</span>, <span class="str">"Awful movie"</span>, <span class="str">"OK plot"</span>, <span class="str">"Loved it"</span>, <span class="str">"Boring"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"author_id"</span>: [<span class="num">101</span>, <span class="num">102</span>, <span class="num">101</span>, <span class="num">103</span>, <span class="num">104</span>]\n})\n\nauthors = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"author_id"</span>: [<span class="num">101</span>, <span class="num">102</span>, <span class="num">103</span>, <span class="num">105</span>],\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>: [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>, <span class="str">"Eve"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"country"</span>: [<span class="str">"US"</span>, <span class="str">"UK"</span>, <span class="str">"TR"</span>, <span class="str">"DE"</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># Inner join -- only matching author_ids</span>\ninner = pd.<span class="fn">merge</span>(texts, authors, on=<span class="str">"author_id"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(inner)  <span class="cm"># 4 rows (author 104 dropped, Eve dropped)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Left join -- keep all texts, fill NaN for missing author</span>\nleft = pd.<span class="fn">merge</span>(texts, authors, on=<span class="str">"author_id"</span>, how=<span class="str">"left"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(left)   <span class="cm"># 5 rows -- "Boring" has NaN for name/country</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Outer join -- everything from both</span>\nouter = pd.<span class="fn">merge</span>(texts, authors, on=<span class="str">"author_id"</span>, how=<span class="str">"outer"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(outer)  <span class="cm"># 6 rows -- includes unmatched from both sides</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Different key column names</strong> -- when tables use different names for the same concept:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># left_on / right_on when column names differ</span>\nreviews = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="str">"review_id"</span>: [<span class="num">1</span>,<span class="num">2</span>], <span class="str">"user"</span>: [<span class="str">"A"</span>,<span class="str">"B"</span>], <span class="str">"text"</span>: [<span class="str">"good"</span>,<span class="str">"bad"</span>]})\nusers = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="str">"username"</span>: [<span class="str">"A"</span>,<span class="str">"B"</span>,<span class="str">"C"</span>], <span class="str">"age"</span>: [<span class="num">25</span>,<span class="num">30</span>,<span class="num">22</span>]})\n\nresult = pd.<span class="fn">merge</span>(reviews, users, left_on=<span class="str">"user"</span>, right_on=<span class="str">"username"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(result)\n<span class="cm">#    review_id user  text username  age</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0          1    A  good        A   25</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1          2    B   bad        B   30</span>\n<span class="cm"># Note: both \'user\' and \'username\' columns kept</span>\nresult = result.<span class="fn">drop</span>(columns=[<span class="str">"username"</span>])  <span class="cm"># remove duplicate</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Merge on index instead of column</span>\ndf_indexed = users.<span class="fn">set_index</span>(<span class="str">"username"</span>)\nresult = pd.<span class="fn">merge</span>(reviews, df_indexed, left_on=<span class="str">"user"</span>, right_index=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Suffixes for overlapping column names</span>\ndf1 = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="str">"id"</span>: [<span class="num">1</span>,<span class="num">2</span>], <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">80</span>,<span class="num">90</span>]})\ndf2 = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="str">"id"</span>: [<span class="num">1</span>,<span class="num">2</span>], <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">85</span>,<span class="num">92</span>]})\nmerged = pd.<span class="fn">merge</span>(df1, df2, on=<span class="str">"id"</span>, suffixes=(<span class="str">"_exam1"</span>, <span class="str">"_exam2"</span>))\n<span class="fn">print</span>(merged)\n<span class="cm">#    id  score_exam1  score_exam2</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0   1           80           85</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1   2           90           92</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps"><div class="steps-title">Merge Checklist -- Avoid Silent Bugs</div><div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Check key uniqueness</strong> -- if the key column has duplicates in BOTH tables, you get a Cartesian product (row explosion). Use <code>df["key"].is_unique</code> before merging.</div></div><div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Verify row count</strong> after merge -- <code>assert len(merged) == expected</code>. If the count changes unexpectedly, you have duplicate keys or a wrong join type.</div></div><div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Use validate parameter</strong> -- <code>pd.merge(..., validate="many_to_one")</code> raises an error if the assumption is violated. Options: "one_to_one", "one_to_many", "many_to_one", "many_to_many".</div></div><div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Check for NaN</strong> after outer/left joins -- <code>merged.isna().sum()</code>. NaN means unmatched keys, which may indicate data quality issues.</div></div></div>'

