+ '<h2 class="l-title">3. Join, combine_first & update</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Three more tools for combining DataFrames:</strong> <code>.join()</code> is a convenience wrapper around merge for index-based joins. <code>combine_first()</code> fills NaN values from another DataFrame. <code>update()</code> overwrites non-NaN values in place. Each solves a different combination scenario.</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># --- .join() -- merge on index ---</span>\ndf_left = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">88</span>, <span class="num">92</span>, <span class="num">75</span>]\n}, index=[<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>])\n\ndf_right = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"age"</span>: [<span class="num">25</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">28</span>, <span class="num">35</span>]\n}, index=[<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>, <span class="str">"Dave"</span>])\n\n<span class="cm"># .join() defaults to left join on index</span>\njoined = df_left.<span class="fn">join</span>(df_right)  <span class="cm"># 3 rows (Dave excluded)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(joined)\n<span class="cm">#        score  age</span>\n<span class="cm"># Alice     88   25</span>\n<span class="cm"># Bob       92   30</span>\n<span class="cm"># Carol     75   28</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Outer join to keep Dave</span>\njoined_outer = df_left.<span class="fn">join</span>(df_right, how=<span class="str">"outer"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(joined_outer)\n<span class="cm">#        score   age</span>\n<span class="cm"># Alice   88.0    25</span>\n<span class="cm"># Bob     92.0    30</span>\n<span class="cm"># Carol   75.0    28</span>\n<span class="cm"># Dave     NaN    35</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Join multiple DataFrames at önce</span>\ndf_extra = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="str">"field"</span>: [<span class="str">"NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"CV"</span>]}, index=[<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>])\njoined_multi = df_left.<span class="fn">join</span>([df_right, df_extra])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(joined_multi)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>combine_first()</strong> -- patching missing values from a backup source:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># combine_first: fill NaN from another DataFrame</span>\nprimary = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>: [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, np.nan],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">88</span>, np.nan, <span class="num">75</span>]\n})\nbackup = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>: [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">85</span>, <span class="num">90</span>, <span class="num">78</span>]\n})\n\nresult = primary.<span class="fn">combine_first</span>(backup)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(result)\n<span class="cm">#     name  score</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0  Alice   88.0   &lt;-- kept from primary</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1    Bob   90.0   &lt;-- filled from backup</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2  Carol   75.0   &lt;-- name from backup, score from primary</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># --- update() -- overwrite in place ---</span>\nbase = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="str">"A"</span>: [<span class="num">1</span>, np.nan, <span class="num">3</span>], <span class="str">"B"</span>: [<span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">6</span>]})\nnew_vals = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="str">"A"</span>: [<span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">20</span>, <span class="num">30</span>]})\n\nbase.<span class="fn">update</span>(new_vals)  <span class="cm"># modifies base IN PLACE</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(base)\n<span class="cm">#       A  B</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0  10.0  4   &lt;-- overwritten</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1  20.0  5   &lt;-- NaN replaced</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2  30.0  6   &lt;-- overwritten</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare"><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">merge()</div><div class="compare-item">Joins by column values (like SQL)</div><div class="compare-item">Supports all 4 join types</div><div class="compare-item">Can match on multiple keys</div><div class="compare-item">Returns a new DataFrame</div><div class="compare-item">Use when: joining by key columns</div></div><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">.join()</div><div class="compare-item">Joins by index (default)</div><div class="compare-item">Shortcut for merge on index</div><div class="compare-item">Supports joining multiple DFs at önce</div><div class="compare-item">Returns a new DataFrame</div><div class="compare-item">Use when: indices are meaningful keys</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>merge vs join vs concat:</strong> Use <code>concat</code> when stacking identical schemas (same columns, different rows). Use <code>merge</code> when matching by key columns (SQL-style). Use <code>join</code> when matching by index. If unsure, use <code>merge</code> -- it is the most general and explicit.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 4: Melt & Pivot
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">4. Reshaping: Melt & Pivot</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Wide vs. Long format:</strong> Wide tables have one row per observation and many columns (each measurement gets its own column). Long tables have one row per measurement (repeated observation IDs, a "variable" column, and a "value" column). <strong>ML models almost always want long or matrix format.</strong> Melt converts wide-to-long; pivot converts long-to-wide.</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards"><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">pd.melt (wide \u2192 long)</div><div class="card-body">Unpivots columns into rows. Turns column headers into values. Essential for tidy data format. The inverse of pivot.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">df.pivot (long \u2192 wide)</div><div class="card-body">Spreads unique values from a column into new columns. Requires unique index-column pairs. The inverse of melt.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">stack / unstack</div><div class="card-body">Pivot with MultiIndex. Stack moves column level into row index. Unstack moves row index level into columns. Lower-level than melt/pivot.</div></div><div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">pivot_table</div><div class="card-body">Like pivot but handles duplicate entries with aggregation functions (mean, sum, count). More robust than plain pivot.</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># --- pd.melt: Wide \u2192 Long ---</span>\nwide = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"student"</span>: [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"math"</span>: [<span class="num">88</span>, <span class="num">72</span>, <span class="num">95</span>],\n    <span class="str">"nlp"</span>: [<span class="num">92</span>, <span class="num">85</span>, <span class="num">78</span>],\n    <span class="str">"dl"</span>: [<span class="num">90</span>, <span class="num">80</span>, <span class="num">88</span>]\n})\n<span class="fn">print</span>(wide)\n<span class="cm">#   student  math  nlp  dl</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0   Alice    88   92  90</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1     Bob    72   85  80</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2   Carol    95   78  88</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Melt: move score columns into rows</span>\nlong = pd.<span class="fn">melt</span>(\n    wide,\n    id_vars=[<span class="str">"student"</span>],       <span class="cm"># keep as identifier column</span>\n    value_vars=[<span class="str">"math"</span>, <span class="str">"nlp"</span>, <span class="str">"dl"</span>],  <span class="cm"># columns to unpivot</span>\n    var_name=<span class="str">"subject"</span>,        <span class="cm"># name for the variable column</span>\n    value_name=<span class="str">"score"</span>         <span class="cm"># name for the value column</span>\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(long)\n<span class="cm">#   student subject  score</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0   Alice    math     88</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1     Bob    math     72</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2   Carol    math     95</span>\n<span class="cm"># 3   Alice     nlp     92</span>\n<span class="cm"># 4     Bob     nlp     85</span>\n<span class="cm"># 5   Carol     nlp     78</span>\n<span class="cm"># 6   Alice      dl     90</span>\n<span class="cm"># 7     Bob      dl     80</span>\n<span class="cm"># 8   Carol      dl     88</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># --- df.pivot: Long \u2192 Wide ---</span>\nback_to_wide = long.<span class="fn">pivot</span>(\n    index=<span class="str">"student"</span>,     <span class="cm"># row identifier</span>\n    columns=<span class="str">"subject"</span>,   <span class="cm"># values become columns</span>\n    values=<span class="str">"score"</span>       <span class="cm"># cell values</span>\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(back_to_wide)\n<span class="cm"># subject    dl  math  nlp</span>\n<span class="cm"># student</span>\n<span class="cm"># Alice      90    88   92</span>\n<span class="cm"># Bob        80    72   85</span>\n<span class="cm"># Carol      88    95   78</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>pivot_table</strong> -- handles duplicates with aggregation:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># pivot() fails if there are duplicate index-column pairs</span>\n<span class="cm"># pivot_table() handles them with aggfunc</span>\nexams = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"student"</span>: [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>,<span class="str">"Alice"</span>,<span class="str">"Bob"</span>,<span class="str">"Bob"</span>,<span class="str">"Alice"</span>,<span class="str">"Bob"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"subject"</span>: [<span class="str">"math"</span>,<span class="str">"math"</span>,<span class="str">"math"</span>,<span class="str">"nlp"</span>,<span class="str">"nlp"</span>,<span class="str">"nlp"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">85</span>,<span class="num">90</span>,<span class="num">78</span>,<span class="num">88</span>,<span class="num">92</span>,<span class="num">82</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># Average score per student per subject</span>\npt = pd.<span class="fn">pivot_table</span>(\n    exams,\n    index=<span class="str">"student"</span>,\n    columns=<span class="str">"subject"</span>,\n    values=<span class="str">"score"</span>,\n    aggfunc=<span class="str">"mean"</span>       <span class="cm"># handles duplicate Alice-math</span>\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(pt)\n<span class="cm"># subject   math   nlp</span>\n<span class="cm"># student</span>\n<span class="cm"># Alice     87.5  92.0</span>\n<span class="cm"># Bob       78.0  85.0</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># --- stack / unstack ---</span>\n<span class="cm"># stack: columns \u2192 inner row index level</span>\nstacked = pt.<span class="fn">stack</span>()\n<span class="fn">print</span>(stacked)\n<span class="cm"># student  subject</span>\n<span class="cm"># Alice    math       87.5</span>\n<span class="cm">#          nlp        92.0</span>\n<span class="cm"># Bob      math       78.0</span>\n<span class="cm">#          nlp        85.0</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># unstack: inner row index level \u2192 columns</span>\nunstacked = stacked.<span class="fn">unstack</span>()\n<span class="fn">print</span>(unstacked)  <span class="cm"># back to pivot table form</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">PRACTICAL: Preparing NLP Data for ML</div><div class="example-body"><strong>Scenario:</strong> You have a wide-format survey where each column is a question (Q1, Q2, ..., Q50) and each row is a respondent. Your model needs long format (respondent_id, question, answer).<br><br><code>long = pd.melt(survey, id_vars=["respondent_id", "age", "gender"], var_name="question", value_name="answer")</code><br><br>Now you can group by question, filter by answer, and feed the tidy data into your pipeline. <strong>Tidy data = each observation is a row, each variable is a column.</strong></div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 5: Advanced Reshaping
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">5. Advanced Reshaping</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Feature engineering through reshaping:</strong> Real ML pipelines need one-hot encoding, list explosion, and JSON normalization. These operations convert raw data into the numeric matrix format that models consume. Pandas provides dedicated functions for each pattern.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>pd.get_dummies()</strong> -- one-hot encoding categorical features:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># --- One-hot encoding ---</span>\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"text"</span>: [<span class="str">"hello world"</span>, <span class="str">"goodbye"</span>, <span class="str">"hi there"</span>, <span class="str">"bye bye"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"sentiment"</span>: [<span class="str">"positive"</span>, <span class="str">"negative"</span>, <span class="str">"positive"</span>, <span class="str">"negative"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"source"</span>: [<span class="str">"twitter"</span>, <span class="str">"reddit"</span>, <span class="str">"twitter"</span>, <span class="str">"review"</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># One-hot encode the source column</span>\ndummies = pd.<span class="fn">get_dummies</span>(df, columns=[<span class="str">"source"</span>], prefix=<span class="str">"src"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(dummies)\n<span class="cm">#           text sentiment  src_reddit  src_review  src_twitter</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0  hello world  positive       False       False         True</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1      goodbye  negative        True       False        False</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2     hi there  positive       False       False         True</span>\n<span class="cm"># 3      bye bye  negative       False        True        False</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># drop_first to avoid multicollinearity (k-1 encoding)</span>\ndummies_k1 = pd.<span class="fn">get_dummies</span>(df, columns=[<span class="str">"source"</span>], drop_first=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Convert bool to int for sklearn</span>\ndummies_int = pd.<span class="fn">get_dummies</span>(df, columns=[<span class="str">"source"</span>], dtype=<span class="fn">int</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(dummies_int[[<span class="str">"src_reddit"</span>,<span class="str">"src_review"</span>,<span class="str">"src_twitter"</span>]])\n<span class="cm">#    src_reddit  src_review  src_twitter</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0           0           0            1</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1           1           0            0</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2           0           0            1</span>\n<span class="cm"># 3           0           1            0</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>explode()</strong> -- expanding lists into rows:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># --- explode: list values \u2192 separate rows ---</span>\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"doc_id"</span>: [<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>],\n    <span class="str">"text"</span>: [<span class="str">"I love NLP"</span>, <span class="str">"Deep learning rocks"</span>, <span class="str">"Pandas is great"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"tags"</span>: [[<span class="str">"nlp"</span>, <span class="str">"ml"</span>], [<span class="str">"dl"</span>, <span class="str">"neural"</span>, <span class="str">"ml"</span>], [<span class="str">"data"</span>]]\n})\n\nexploded = df.<span class="fn">explode</span>(<span class="str">"tags"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(exploded)\n<span class="cm">#    doc_id                 text    tags</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0       1           I love NLP     nlp</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0       1           I love NLP      ml</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1       2  Deep learning rocks      dl</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1       2  Deep learning rocks  neural</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1       2  Deep learning rocks      ml</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2       3      Pandas is great    data</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Now you can groupby tags, count, or one-hot encode</span>\ntag_counts = exploded.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="str">"tags"</span>).<span class="fn">size</span>().<span class="fn">sort_values</span>(ascending=<span class="kw">False</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(tag_counts)\n<span class="cm"># ml        2</span>\n<span class="cm"># nlp       1</span>\n<span class="cm"># dl        1</span>\n<span class="cm"># neural    1</span>\n<span class="cm"># data      1</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>pd.json_normalize()</strong> -- flattening nested JSON/dicts:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># --- json_normalize: nested dicts \u2192 flat DataFrame ---</span>\napi_data = [\n    {\n        <span class="str">"id"</span>: <span class="num">1</span>,\n        <span class="str">"text"</span>: <span class="str">"Great movie"</span>,\n        <span class="str">"metadata"</span>: {<span class="str">"source"</span>: <span class="str">"twitter"</span>, <span class="str">"lang"</span>: <span class="str">"en"</span>},\n        <span class="str">"scores"</span>: {<span class="str">"positive"</span>: <span class="num">0.92</span>, <span class="str">"negative"</span>: <span class="num">0.08</span>}\n    },\n    {\n        <span class="str">"id"</span>: <span class="num">2</span>,\n        <span class="str">"text"</span>: <span class="str">"Terrible film"</span>,\n        <span class="str">"metadata"</span>: {<span class="str">"source"</span>: <span class="str">"reddit"</span>, <span class="str">"lang"</span>: <span class="str">"en"</span>},\n        <span class="str">"scores"</span>: {<span class="str">"positive"</span>: <span class="num">0.15</span>, <span class="str">"negative"</span>: <span class="num">0.85</span>}\n    }\n]\n\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">json_normalize</span>(api_data)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.columns.<span class="fn">tolist</span>())\n<span class="cm"># [\'id\', \'text\', \'metadata.source\', \'metadata.lang\',</span>\n<span class="cm">#  \'scores.positive\', \'scores.negative\']</span>\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df)\n<span class="cm">#    id           text metadata.source metadata.lang  scores.positive  scores.negative</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0   1    Great movie         twitter            en             0.92             0.08</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1   2  Terrible film          reddit            en             0.15             0.85</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Custom separator for column names</span>\ndf2 = pd.<span class="fn">json_normalize</span>(api_data, sep=<span class="str">"_"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df2.columns.<span class="fn">tolist</span>())\n<span class="cm"># [\'id\', \'text\', \'metadata_source\', \'metadata_lang\',</span>\n<span class="cm">#  \'scores_positive\', \'scores_negative\']</span></code></pre></div>'

