+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> After melting a wide table (3 students x 3 subjects), each student-subject combination becomes its own row. The original 3 rows with 4 columns become 9 rows with 3 columns (student, subject, score). This long format is what most ML libraries and visualization tools expect.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><div class="think-label">THINK ABOUT IT</div><div class="think-body"><strong>One-hot encoding and the dummy variable trap:</strong> If a categorical column has k categories, you only need k-1 dummy columns. Including all k creates <strong>perfect multicollinearity</strong> -- the last column is perfectly predictable from the others. Use <code>drop_first=True</code> for linear models (linear/logistic regression). Tree-based models (Random Forest, XGBoost) are not affected, so you can keep all k columns for them.</div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 6: Complete Data Pipeline
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">6. Complete Data Pipeline</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Putting it all together:</strong> A real data pipeline loads multiple CSVs, merges them on shared keys, cleans the result, reshapes for analysis, engineers features, and outputs an ML-ready matrix. This section walks through a complete example connecting everything from this course.</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps"><div class="steps-title">Pipeline: Multiple CSVs \u2192 ML-Ready Dataset</div><div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Load & Concat</strong> -- Read multiple monthly CSV files and stack them vertically. Reset index to avoid duplicates.</div></div><div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Merge</strong> -- Enrich the combined data by joining with metadata tables (user info, category descriptions) using shared keys.</div></div><div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Clean</strong> -- Handle missing values (dropna/fillna), remove duplicates, fix dtypes, standardize text (lowercase, strip whitespace).</div></div><div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Reshape</strong> -- Melt wide features if needed, explode list columns, normalize nested JSON fields into flat columns.</div></div><div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Feature Engineer</strong> -- Create text features (word_count, char_count), one-hot encode categoricals, bin numerical features.</div></div><div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">6</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Export</strong> -- Save as parquet (fast, typed) or feed directly into scikit-learn / PyTorch DataLoader.</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># ========== STEP 1: Load & Concat ==========</span>\n<span class="cm"># Simulate loading multiple monthly review files</span>\njan = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"review_id"</span>: [<span class="num">1</span>,<span class="num">2</span>,<span class="num">3</span>],\n    <span class="str">"user_id"</span>: [<span class="num">101</span>,<span class="num">102</span>,<span class="num">103</span>],\n    <span class="str">"text"</span>: [<span class="str">"Amazing product!"</span>, <span class="str">"Not worth it"</span>, <span class="str">"Pretty good overall"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"rating"</span>: [<span class="num">5</span>,<span class="num">1</span>,<span class="num">4</span>]\n})\nfeb = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"review_id"</span>: [<span class="num">4</span>,<span class="num">5</span>,<span class="num">6</span>,<span class="num">7</span>],\n    <span class="str">"user_id"</span>: [<span class="num">101</span>,<span class="num">104</span>,<span class="num">105</span>,<span class="num">102</span>],\n    <span class="str">"text"</span>: [<span class="str">"Still love it"</span>, <span class="str">"Terrible quality"</span>, <span class="str">"OK for the price"</span>, <span class="str">"Broke after a week"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"rating"</span>: [<span class="num">5</span>,<span class="num">1</span>,<span class="num">3</span>,<span class="num">1</span>]\n})\n\nreviews = pd.<span class="fn">concat</span>([jan, feb], ignore_index=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Combined: {reviews.shape}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># (7, 4)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># ========== STEP 2: Merge with user metadata ==========</span>\nusers = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"user_id"</span>: [<span class="num">101</span>,<span class="num">102</span>,<span class="num">103</span>,<span class="num">104</span>,<span class="num">105</span>,<span class="num">106</span>],\n    <span class="str">"country"</span>: [<span class="str">"US"</span>,<span class="str">"UK"</span>,<span class="str">"TR"</span>,<span class="str">"DE"</span>,<span class="str">"FR"</span>,<span class="str">"JP"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"signup_year"</span>: [<span class="num">2020</span>,<span class="num">2021</span>,<span class="num">2019</span>,<span class="num">2022</span>,<span class="num">2023</span>,<span class="num">2021</span>]\n})\n\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">merge</span>(reviews, users, on=<span class="str">"user_id"</span>, how=<span class="str">"left"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"After merge: {df.shape}"</span>)   <span class="cm"># (7, 6)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">isna</span>().<span class="fn">sum</span>())  <span class="cm"># check for unmatched users</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># ========== STEP 3: Clean ==========</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"text"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"text"</span>].str.<span class="fn">lower</span>().str.<span class="fn">strip</span>()\ndf = df.<span class="fn">drop_duplicates</span>(subset=[<span class="str">"text"</span>])\ndf[<span class="str">"rating"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"rating"</span>].<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="str">"int8"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ========== STEP 4: Feature Engineering ==========</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"word_count"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"text"</span>].<span class="fn">apply</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span> x: <span class="fn">len</span>(x.<span class="fn">split</span>()))\ndf[<span class="str">"char_count"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"text"</span>].str.<span class="fn">len</span>()\ndf[<span class="str">"has_exclaim"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"text"</span>].str.<span class="fn">contains</span>(<span class="str">"!"</span>).<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="fn">int</span>)\ndf[<span class="str">"sentiment"</span>] = (df[<span class="str">"rating"</span>] >= <span class="num">4</span>).<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="fn">int</span>)  <span class="cm"># binary label</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># One-hot encode country</span>\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">get_dummies</span>(df, columns=[<span class="str">"country"</span>], prefix=<span class="str">"c"</span>, dtype=<span class="fn">int</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ========== STEP 5: Prepare for ML ==========</span>\nfeature_cols = [<span class="str">"word_count"</span>, <span class="str">"char_count"</span>, <span class="str">"has_exclaim"</span>] + \\\n    [c <span class="kw">for</span> c <span class="kw">in</span> df.columns <span class="kw">if</span> c.<span class="fn">startswith</span>(<span class="str">"c_"</span>)]\n\nX = df[feature_cols].values   <span class="cm"># NumPy array for sklearn</span>\ny = df[<span class="str">"sentiment"</span>].values    <span class="cm"># target labels</span>\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"X shape: {X.shape}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># (7, 8) -- 5 numerical + country dummies</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"y shape: {y.shape}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># (7,)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"ML-ready!"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ========== STEP 6: Save ==========</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">to_parquet</span>(<span class="str">"reviews_processed.parquet"</span>)  <span class="cm"># fast, typed, compressed</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">to_csv</span>(<span class="str">"reviews_processed.csv"</span>, index=<span class="kw">False</span>)  <span class="cm"># universal</span></code></pre></div>'

