+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> Each pipeline stage transforms the dataset shape. Loading starts with 3 rows (one file), concat doubles to 7 rows, merge adds 2 columns (country, signup_year), feature engineering adds more columns (word_count, char_count, etc.), and one-hot encoding expands categorical columns into binary indicators. The final ML-ready dataset has all numeric features plus a binary target.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">FULL EXAMPLE: NLP Sentiment Pipeline</div><div class="example-body"><strong>Real-world scenario:</strong> You have 12 monthly CSV files of customer reviews, a user database, and a product catalog. Your goal is a sentiment classifier.<br><br><strong>Step 1:</strong> <code>dfs = [pd.read_csv(f"reviews_{m}.csv") for m in months]</code><br><code>all_reviews = pd.concat(dfs, ignore_index=True)</code><br><br><strong>Step 2:</strong> <code>df = pd.merge(all_reviews, users, on="user_id", how="left")</code><br><code>df = pd.merge(df, products, on="product_id", how="left")</code><br><br><strong>Step 3:</strong> <code>df["text"] = df["text"].str.lower().str.strip()</code><br><code>df = df.dropna(subset=["text", "rating"])</code><br><br><strong>Step 4:</strong> <code>df["word_count"] = df["text"].str.split().str.len()</code><br><code>df = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=["category"], dtype=int)</code><br><br><strong>Step 5:</strong> <code>X = df[feature_cols].values; y = (df["rating"] >= 4).astype(int).values</code><br><code>df.to_parquet("sentiment_data.parquet")</code><br><br><strong>Result:</strong> Clean, merged, feature-engineered dataset ready for sklearn or PyTorch.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><div class="think-label">KEY TAKEAWAYS</div><div class="think-body"><strong>1.</strong> <code>pd.concat()</code> stacks DataFrames vertically (rows) or horizontally (columns) -- no key matching, just gluing.<br><strong>2.</strong> <code>pd.merge()</code> is SQL-style joining -- inner, left, right, outer. Always verify row count and check for NaN after merging.<br><strong>3.</strong> <code>.join()</code> is a shortcut for index-based merges. <code>combine_first()</code> patches NaN values. <code>update()</code> overwrites in place.<br><strong>4.</strong> <code>pd.melt()</code> converts wide to long (columns become rows). <code>df.pivot()</code> converts long to wide (row values become columns).<br><strong>5.</strong> <code>pd.get_dummies()</code> one-hot encodes categoricals. <code>explode()</code> expands lists. <code>json_normalize()</code> flattens nested dicts.<br><strong>6.</strong> A complete pipeline is: load \u2192 concat \u2192 merge \u2192 clean \u2192 reshape \u2192 feature engineer \u2192 export. Each step is one or two Pandas calls.</div></div>'

